1
00:00:08,340 --> 00:00:10,801
[WILLIAM] It sounds like
a delightful diversion.

2
00:00:10,843 --> 00:00:13,470
Yes, and I shall
be heavily reliant

3
00:00:13,512 --> 00:00:15,723
upon your insight
on this matter.

4
00:00:15,765 --> 00:00:18,392
And your expertise, William.

5
00:00:18,434 --> 00:00:19,810
Naturally, yes.

6
00:00:19,852 --> 00:00:23,856
You did set this in motion, so
you'll take the lead, Albert.

7
00:00:23,898 --> 00:00:26,776
[ALBERT] Louis, I expect
much of you, as well.

8
00:00:26,817 --> 00:00:28,944
Yes, brother.

9
00:00:28,985 --> 00:00:31,614
We share not only the same goal.

10
00:00:31,655 --> 00:00:36,243
But also past,
present, and future.

11
00:00:36,285 --> 00:00:40,790
Our lives entwined by choice.

12
00:00:40,831 --> 00:00:42,833
For we three brothers

13
00:00:42,875 --> 00:00:45,920
are the singular
"James Moriarty."

14
00:02:23,308 --> 00:02:24,935
[SERVER 13A]
We ask you to please refrain

15
00:02:24,977 --> 00:02:27,521
from speaking anyone's
name whilst inside.

16
00:02:27,563 --> 00:02:32,401
Do bear in mind, this is
meant to be a masquerade ball.

17
00:02:32,442 --> 00:02:35,195
Also, as part of this
evening's entertainment,

18
00:02:35,237 --> 00:02:38,032
we will be playing
a riddle-solving game.

19
00:02:38,073 --> 00:02:39,700
The theme of which

20
00:02:39,742 --> 00:02:43,913
is the Lord of Crime.
The current talk of the town.

21
00:02:43,954 --> 00:02:46,040
This mysterious
figure is rumoured

22
00:02:46,081 --> 00:02:48,458
to have his hand in nearly
half of the criminal activity

23
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:51,253
that has taken place
in London of late.

24
00:02:51,294 --> 00:02:52,796
The rules are simple:

25
00:02:52,838 --> 00:02:54,006
one of our esteemed guests

26
00:02:54,048 --> 00:02:56,592
is chosen to play
the Lord of Crime.

27
00:02:56,633 --> 00:03:00,971
And that person will
commit one or more murders.

28
00:03:01,013 --> 00:03:05,017
Any guest who is murdered must
reveal their face to the rest.

29
00:03:05,059 --> 00:03:08,854
And then kindly
relocate to the upper floor.

30
00:03:08,896 --> 00:03:09,980
Following that,

31
00:03:10,022 --> 00:03:12,148
they will no longer be
able to attend the ball.

32
00:03:12,190 --> 00:03:15,027
And must donate the most
costly item adorning them

33
00:03:15,069 --> 00:03:17,529
towards the college
currently under construction

34
00:03:17,571 --> 00:03:19,740
as a penalty for
losing the game.

35
00:03:19,782 --> 00:03:21,157
Ah.

36
00:03:21,199 --> 00:03:23,243
[SERVER 13A] Further,
once the remaining players

37
00:03:23,285 --> 00:03:25,537
determine the
Lord of Crime's identity,

38
00:03:25,579 --> 00:03:26,956
the villain will die.

39
00:03:26,997 --> 00:03:29,249
And the game
will come to an end.

40
00:03:29,291 --> 00:03:32,836
We shall have to do
our utmost to find them.

41
00:03:32,878 --> 00:03:36,339
Yes. It sounds exhilarating.

42
00:03:44,681 --> 00:03:45,891
It's my understanding

43
00:03:45,933 --> 00:03:49,436
that the Yard has no clues
as to his identity so far.

44
00:03:49,478 --> 00:03:51,855
Some of the common-folk
call the Lord of Crime a hero

45
00:03:51,897 --> 00:03:54,984
set on bringing the dark deeds
of the upper class to light.

46
00:03:55,025 --> 00:03:56,401
[NOBLEMAN 13C]
As things stand,

47
00:03:56,443 --> 00:03:58,988
he is apparently as well-liked
as the Great Detective Holmes,

48
00:03:59,029 --> 00:04:02,825
as a sort of symbol
representing light and darkness.

49
00:04:02,866 --> 00:04:05,201
The rumour mill
is certainly churning.

50
00:04:05,243 --> 00:04:08,038
Appropriate for the ruler
of London's underworld.

51
00:04:08,080 --> 00:04:11,625
Bah! What rubbish!
The "Lord of Crime" indeed.

52
00:04:11,667 --> 00:04:15,754
A petty criminal, getting
the Robin Hood treatment?

53
00:04:15,796 --> 00:04:18,214
[ALBERT]
It's no surprise.

54
00:04:18,256 --> 00:04:19,925
The noblemen who were killed

55
00:04:19,967 --> 00:04:22,552
were embroiled in a
variety of scandals.

56
00:04:22,594 --> 00:04:24,262
Naturally,
the townsfolk look upon

57
00:04:24,304 --> 00:04:27,557
the Lord of Crime
as a valiant thief.

58
00:04:27,599 --> 00:04:28,892
Hah.

59
00:04:28,934 --> 00:04:30,978
It sounds like you're saying
their deaths were justified.

60
00:04:31,020 --> 00:04:33,396
Very uncouth, sir.

61
00:04:33,438 --> 00:04:35,732
No, nothing of the sort.

62
00:04:35,774 --> 00:04:37,442
[ROLLINSON]
Who is this young chap?

63
00:04:37,484 --> 00:04:40,403
Quite the disagreeable twit.

64
00:04:40,445 --> 00:04:42,781
[ROLLINSON] Hmph. The fool
doesn't even realize

65
00:04:42,823 --> 00:04:45,575
that I was chosen to
be the Lord of Crime.

66
00:04:45,617 --> 00:04:50,455
That settles it; my very
first victim will be him.

67
00:05:14,563 --> 00:05:16,690
Is something amiss, my lord?

68
00:05:16,732 --> 00:05:18,483
No.

69
00:05:18,525 --> 00:05:21,570
Pardon! May I have
your attention, please?

70
00:05:21,611 --> 00:05:23,655
Regarding tonight's
game of intrigue,

71
00:05:23,697 --> 00:05:25,657
our Lord of Crime's
first heinous act

72
00:05:25,699 --> 00:05:28,202
has already been committed.

73
00:05:29,494 --> 00:05:33,582
The fiend's first victim will
be marked with red wine.

74
00:05:33,623 --> 00:05:35,000
Anyone who has had wine

75
00:05:35,042 --> 00:05:36,877
splashed on them
has been murdered.

76
00:05:36,919 --> 00:05:39,963
Take this time to
inspect those around you.

77
00:05:40,005 --> 00:05:41,506
Did he say wine?

78
00:05:41,548 --> 00:05:43,800
I've survived thus far,
it would seem.

79
00:05:43,842 --> 00:05:46,720
Would you be so kind
as to check my back?

80
00:05:47,971 --> 00:05:50,515
[NOBLEWOMAN 13A gasps]
Oh, dear!

81
00:05:51,683 --> 00:05:56,396
Well, it would appear that
I am the killer's first victim.

82
00:05:56,438 --> 00:05:58,982
[NOBLEWOMAN 13C]
Not Lord Moriarty!

83
00:05:59,024 --> 00:06:00,275
[NOBLEWOMAN 13D]
Who would splash wine on

84
00:06:00,316 --> 00:06:03,987
such a fine young man?

85
00:06:04,029 --> 00:06:08,324
I was Earl Albert James
Moriarty, before my death!

86
00:06:08,366 --> 00:06:11,620
[NOBLEWOMAN 13E]
No! Lord Moriarty!

87
00:06:11,661 --> 00:06:16,917
Ah, yes, that's right.
I shall be donating this ring.

88
00:06:16,959 --> 00:06:21,504
[SERVER 13A] Thank you, my lord.
Such a generous offering.

89
00:06:21,546 --> 00:06:25,926
[ROLLINSON] I never expected
someone so young to be an earl.

90
00:06:25,968 --> 00:06:27,136
Ladies and gentlemen,

91
00:06:27,177 --> 00:06:29,012
a round of applause
for His Lordship!

92
00:06:29,054 --> 00:06:31,556
[CROWD clapping, cheers]

93
00:06:31,598 --> 00:06:33,058
[ADLER]
I, as well.

94
00:06:33,100 --> 00:06:34,559
Huh?

95
00:06:34,601 --> 00:06:35,894
It would appear that I, too,

96
00:06:35,936 --> 00:06:39,189
have fallen victim
to the Lord of Crime!

97
00:06:39,231 --> 00:06:40,356
[ROLLINSON grunts]

98
00:06:40,398 --> 00:06:43,526
[ROLLINSON] I don't recall
splashing any on him.

99
00:06:51,827 --> 00:06:53,453
Rather clever of you to adopt

100
00:06:53,495 --> 00:06:56,039
a second persona to
come here tonight,

101
00:06:56,081 --> 00:06:59,126
Miss Irene Adler.

102
00:06:59,168 --> 00:07:00,877
You could have taken me
into your custody

103
00:07:00,919 --> 00:07:03,880
when you bumped into
me at Baker Street.

104
00:07:03,922 --> 00:07:05,381
The fact that you didn't,

105
00:07:05,423 --> 00:07:08,635
shows that you have no wish
to deal roughly with me.

106
00:07:08,677 --> 00:07:12,848
And now, thanks to you
arranging this anonymous affair,

107
00:07:12,889 --> 00:07:14,557
I feel you can be trusted--

108
00:07:14,599 --> 00:07:17,311
someone with whom
I could negotiate.

109
00:07:17,352 --> 00:07:18,979
How very judicious.

110
00:07:19,021 --> 00:07:20,438
I have been charged
with the task

111
00:07:20,480 --> 00:07:22,858
of handling this matter
by the government.

112
00:07:22,899 --> 00:07:24,109
You may freely take my word

113
00:07:24,151 --> 00:07:28,280
to be the very word of
the British authority itself.

114
00:07:28,322 --> 00:07:30,991
The papers you stole
from the palace--

115
00:07:31,033 --> 00:07:33,952
you are willing to
return them to us?

116
00:07:33,994 --> 00:07:37,914
With pleasure, though,
I have some conditions.

117
00:07:37,956 --> 00:07:42,085
Firstly, you must guarantee
my safety from reprisals.

118
00:07:42,127 --> 00:07:46,589
Secondly, the government must
overlook the crime of my theft.

119
00:07:46,631 --> 00:07:48,925
If you will observe
these two conditions,

120
00:07:48,967 --> 00:07:51,053
I will return them at once.

121
00:07:56,432 --> 00:08:01,063
[ALBERT] My answer, on behalf
of the government is no.

122
00:08:01,104 --> 00:08:03,190
[ADLER gasps]

123
00:08:03,232 --> 00:08:07,652
My superiors have afforded me no
room to bargain in this matter.

124
00:08:07,694 --> 00:08:09,196
The orders I was issued were to

125
00:08:09,238 --> 00:08:13,575
seize the documents and
then to liquidate you.

126
00:08:13,616 --> 00:08:16,578
Why did you summon me here
if that was your purpose?

127
00:08:16,619 --> 00:08:19,164
Whilst I serve as an
agent of the government,

128
00:08:19,206 --> 00:08:22,209
it was not in that capacity
that I called on you.

129
00:08:22,251 --> 00:08:24,460
[ADLER gasps]

130
00:08:24,502 --> 00:08:27,463
[ALBERT] I had my
reservations from the outset.

131
00:08:27,505 --> 00:08:29,883
Why did the thief
who stole the documents

132
00:08:29,925 --> 00:08:32,386
choose to do nothing with them?

133
00:08:32,427 --> 00:08:34,137
She gave no demands,

134
00:08:34,179 --> 00:08:38,016
nor did she claim
responsibility for the theft.

135
00:08:38,058 --> 00:08:40,560
Why would that be?

136
00:08:40,602 --> 00:08:42,938
Perhaps because
she realised that

137
00:08:42,979 --> 00:08:45,982
the content of the papers was
beyond what she could handle.

138
00:08:46,024 --> 00:08:49,736
And was desperate to find a
safer means of returning them.

139
00:08:49,778 --> 00:08:51,947
But now, it is
no longer possible

140
00:08:51,988 --> 00:08:53,448
to cast aside the
prospect that

141
00:08:53,489 --> 00:08:55,700
you have learnt some dirty state
secret.

142
00:08:55,742 --> 00:08:58,412
Overlooking your crime and
guaranteeing your safety

143
00:08:58,453 --> 00:09:01,915
in the light of this new
information would be impossible.

144
00:09:01,957 --> 00:09:05,627
If that's true, you've only
come here to inform me directly

145
00:09:05,668 --> 00:09:07,003
- -that I have no hope of any--

146
00:09:07,045 --> 00:09:07,921
- -[ALBERT] No, you're wrong.
- -that I have no hope of any--

147
00:09:07,963 --> 00:09:09,798
- -[ALBERT] No, you're wrong.
- -What?

148
00:09:09,839 --> 00:09:14,177
[ALBERT] I'm here because I just
may have a way to save you.

149
00:09:14,219 --> 00:09:18,098
You'll "save" me?
What do you mean?

150
00:09:18,140 --> 00:09:21,643
[ALBERT] However, my offer is
entirely dependent

151
00:09:21,684 --> 00:09:25,605
on how critical the content of
those papers is to the nation.

152
00:09:25,647 --> 00:09:27,149
[ADLER gasps]
But if you knew,

153
00:09:27,190 --> 00:09:28,817
you would be placed
in the same position

154
00:09:28,858 --> 00:09:30,444
that I've found myself in.

155
00:09:30,485 --> 00:09:33,863
No matter, I can deal
with that problem

156
00:09:33,905 --> 00:09:36,992
once I know what
those papers contain.

157
00:09:38,285 --> 00:09:39,453
I promise you--

158
00:09:39,494 --> 00:09:42,372
you'll regret hearing
what I'm about to tell you.

159
00:09:45,375 --> 00:09:47,085
[ALBERT gasps]

160
00:09:47,127 --> 00:09:48,420
[ADLER grunts]

161
00:09:49,379 --> 00:09:53,008
[ALBERT chuckles]

162
00:09:53,049 --> 00:09:56,845
[ALBERT cackling]

163
00:09:56,886 --> 00:09:59,139
[ALBERT]
It's not about the royal family.

164
00:09:59,181 --> 00:10:04,436
This scandal concerns the entire
British Empire, doesn't it?

165
00:10:04,478 --> 00:10:07,647
And you truly believe you
have the ability to save me?

166
00:10:07,689 --> 00:10:09,983
[ALBERT chuckles]
But of course.

167
00:10:10,025 --> 00:10:12,277
I'm able to save you, and more.

168
00:10:12,319 --> 00:10:14,779
Would you care for a sample?

169
00:10:16,531 --> 00:10:21,661
[ALBERT] Just look at
that man in the white mask.

170
00:10:21,703 --> 00:10:25,332
That is Baron Ronald Rollinson.

171
00:10:25,374 --> 00:10:29,127
The Lord of Crime for
tonight's entertainment.

172
00:10:29,169 --> 00:10:30,545
How do you know that?

173
00:10:30,586 --> 00:10:34,174
Because I am one of the hosts
of this evening's festivities.

174
00:10:34,216 --> 00:10:36,676
But that isn't my point.

175
00:10:36,718 --> 00:10:40,596
The Baron operates opera houses
in both America and France.

176
00:10:40,638 --> 00:10:44,393
His personality is
cruel and self-serving.

177
00:10:44,434 --> 00:10:48,230
Five years ago, he set
fire to one of his theatres.

178
00:10:48,271 --> 00:10:49,898
He made it look
like an accident,

179
00:10:49,939 --> 00:10:51,733
for the insurance money,

180
00:10:51,774 --> 00:10:54,319
killing many of
the people inside.

181
00:10:54,361 --> 00:10:56,988
What? I know all
about that fire.

182
00:10:57,030 --> 00:11:00,200
Some of my friends were
killed in the blaze.

183
00:11:00,242 --> 00:11:03,786
You're telling me
that man's responsible?

184
00:11:03,828 --> 00:11:05,205
[ALBERT]
It's time.

185
00:11:05,247 --> 00:11:09,125
Watch carefully now.

186
00:11:09,167 --> 00:11:10,168
[SERVER 13A]
Attention!

187
00:11:10,210 --> 00:11:13,505
Our Lord of Crime has
been caught at last!

188
00:11:13,547 --> 00:11:15,131
Our villain tonight

189
00:11:15,173 --> 00:11:17,759
has been Baron Ronald Rollinson!

190
00:11:17,800 --> 00:11:19,469
[CROWD claps]

191
00:11:19,511 --> 00:11:21,263
[SERVER 13A]
The Lord of Crime is dead,

192
00:11:21,304 --> 00:11:24,057
and peace returns to
the great city of London!

193
00:11:24,099 --> 00:11:26,809
You are all the winners!

194
00:11:26,851 --> 00:11:29,104
I didn't know it
was you, old boy!

195
00:11:29,145 --> 00:11:32,107
[ROLLINSON wheezes, groans]

196
00:11:32,148 --> 00:11:34,276
Are you all right?
You're looking a bit pale.

197
00:11:34,317 --> 00:11:37,653
[ROLLINSON groaning]

198
00:11:37,695 --> 00:11:39,155
- -[ADLER grunts]
- -[NOBLEMAN 13A] Hamming it up?

199
00:11:39,197 --> 00:11:41,074
[NOBLEMAN 13A]
You can stop acting already.

200
00:11:41,116 --> 00:11:43,368
[ROLLINSON]
C-Can't breathe.

201
00:11:43,410 --> 00:11:45,495
[ROLLINSON groans]

202
00:11:45,537 --> 00:11:46,455
[ADLER gasps]

203
00:11:46,496 --> 00:11:48,790
- -[heart thumps]
- -[ROLLINSON wheezes]

204
00:11:48,831 --> 00:11:50,959
- -[NOBLEWOMAN 13C] Oh!
- -[NOBLEWOMAN 13D] No.

205
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:52,835
[NOBLEMAN 13E]
How dreadful. He looks ghastly!

206
00:11:52,877 --> 00:11:54,003
[NOBLEMAN 13A]
He isn't breathing.

207
00:11:54,045 --> 00:11:56,714
[NOBLEMAN 13C]
Baron? Baron!

208
00:11:56,756 --> 00:11:58,758
Answer me! Hey!

209
00:11:58,800 --> 00:12:00,969
Someone call a doctor!

210
00:12:01,010 --> 00:12:04,264
[ALBERT] His performance--
what'd you think?

211
00:12:04,556 --> 00:12:06,099
[ALBERT]
Baron Rollinson played his part

212
00:12:06,141 --> 00:12:08,684
as the main
character exquisitely.

213
00:12:08,726 --> 00:12:12,772
As did the other nobles
who met their demise.

214
00:12:12,814 --> 00:12:17,735
And now I have pronounced
the same punishment on him.

215
00:12:20,655 --> 00:12:22,240
[ADLER]
It can't be...

216
00:12:22,282 --> 00:12:24,033
Unless he's...

217
00:12:24,075 --> 00:12:29,080
Yes, I set this entire
scene in motion, Miss Adler.

218
00:12:29,122 --> 00:12:34,043
The Lord of Crime, James
Moriarty, at your service.

219
00:12:35,420 --> 00:12:38,673
Dear God, you are him.

220
00:12:38,714 --> 00:12:40,425
Have a look.

221
00:12:44,262 --> 00:12:46,222
[ALBERT]
Even when a man dies,

222
00:12:46,264 --> 00:12:48,224
the aristocracy
continues their party

223
00:12:48,266 --> 00:12:50,018
without a second thought.

224
00:12:50,059 --> 00:12:53,396
Because the fools are
only interested in themselves.

225
00:12:53,438 --> 00:12:54,648
Which means,

226
00:12:54,689 --> 00:12:57,775
no matter how many aristocrats
get killed like this,

227
00:12:57,817 --> 00:13:00,529
there is no changing them.

228
00:13:00,570 --> 00:13:02,489
Aristocracy doesn't refer to

229
00:13:02,531 --> 00:13:04,866
the finely-dressed
people themselves,

230
00:13:04,907 --> 00:13:08,244
but to the very
concept they embody.

231
00:13:09,329 --> 00:13:13,749
[ALBERT] That said, no man can
kill an intangible concept.

232
00:13:15,293 --> 00:13:18,921
Your objective is the
same as mine, is it not?

233
00:13:18,963 --> 00:13:20,340
[ADLER gasps]

234
00:13:20,382 --> 00:13:21,966
My objective is clear:

235
00:13:22,008 --> 00:13:25,428
to fundamentally change
this nation at its core,

236
00:13:25,470 --> 00:13:28,390
to move it from a society
based on right of blood

237
00:13:28,431 --> 00:13:32,143
to a free society
based on merit.

238
00:13:32,185 --> 00:13:34,563
The methodology I use
to bring about reform

239
00:13:34,604 --> 00:13:36,648
in this twisted world

240
00:13:36,690 --> 00:13:39,734
is organized crime.

241
00:13:39,775 --> 00:13:41,903
Just as you yourself
resort to theft,

242
00:13:41,944 --> 00:13:45,615
extortion, and other
crimes as your methods.

243
00:13:48,451 --> 00:13:51,913
[ADLER] Changing the
world through crime.

244
00:13:51,954 --> 00:13:55,124
Maybe he's not so mad after all.

245
00:13:56,167 --> 00:13:59,254
Please forgive me for
speaking at such length.

246
00:13:59,295 --> 00:14:01,715
I will be waiting for you

247
00:14:01,756 --> 00:14:03,883
in the church along
New North Road in Hoxton

248
00:14:03,925 --> 00:14:06,511
just before dawn.

249
00:14:06,553 --> 00:14:07,721
At this point,

250
00:14:07,762 --> 00:14:11,600
you have run out of both
time and further options.

251
00:14:11,641 --> 00:14:15,437
It is my hope you
will choose wisely.

252
00:14:22,235 --> 00:14:25,238
[ADLER]
Lord Albert James Moriarty.

253
00:14:26,322 --> 00:14:29,909
[ADLER] So, the Lord of Crime is
a nobleman who's worked his way

254
00:14:29,951 --> 00:14:32,621
into the heart of the
government, at that.

255
00:14:32,662 --> 00:14:34,247
But the question remains:

256
00:14:34,289 --> 00:14:37,375
does he really have the
power to change anything?

257
00:14:38,334 --> 00:14:40,503
[ADLER gasps]

258
00:14:41,379 --> 00:14:45,258
- -[whip snaps]
- -[HORSES clopping]

259
00:14:47,343 --> 00:14:49,178
[ADLER] First, I need to get the
papers

260
00:14:49,220 --> 00:14:51,973
without Sherlock and the others
noticing.

261
00:14:57,019 --> 00:15:00,607
[ADLER grunting]

262
00:15:00,649 --> 00:15:02,525
My God!

263
00:15:08,448 --> 00:15:10,866
[ADLER gasps]
No.

264
00:15:12,410 --> 00:15:14,036
[ADLER]
Sherlock!

265
00:15:16,289 --> 00:15:19,709
I-Irene, hurry!

266
00:15:19,751 --> 00:15:22,044
He wanted us to hand you over.

267
00:15:22,086 --> 00:15:27,467
I said I didn't know you
and told him to get out of here.

268
00:15:27,509 --> 00:15:30,762
He had the moves of someone
who was extremely well trained

269
00:15:30,804 --> 00:15:33,931
and it took all we
had to turn him back.

270
00:15:33,973 --> 00:15:37,185
I can't believe we didn't
see him plant the bomb.

271
00:15:37,226 --> 00:15:39,521
I'd have sworn he
was only bluffing,

272
00:15:39,562 --> 00:15:41,272
but he meant business.

273
00:15:41,314 --> 00:15:43,483
[groans]

274
00:15:43,525 --> 00:15:44,651
[HOLMES]
You have to--

275
00:15:44,693 --> 00:15:46,444
You have to get out of here.

276
00:15:46,486 --> 00:15:47,612
I couldn't!

277
00:15:47,654 --> 00:15:49,489
You must!

278
00:15:49,531 --> 00:15:51,282
[ADLER grunts]

279
00:15:52,742 --> 00:15:55,537
[ADLER] It's all my fault
they got dragged into this.

280
00:15:55,578 --> 00:15:58,498
I can't let anything
else awful happen to them!

281
00:16:01,042 --> 00:16:02,711
[gasps]

282
00:16:09,300 --> 00:16:10,677
[gasps]

283
00:16:10,719 --> 00:16:11,927
I see.

284
00:16:11,969 --> 00:16:15,014
Thank you for showing me
where you've been hiding it.

285
00:16:15,056 --> 00:16:17,559
Why, Sherlock.
I thought that you--

286
00:16:17,600 --> 00:16:20,311
It worked this time, Irene.

287
00:16:20,353 --> 00:16:22,271
At last, I've won.

288
00:16:22,313 --> 00:16:23,105
[ADLER grunts]

289
00:16:23,147 --> 00:16:24,440
[WATSON]
Honestly,

290
00:16:24,482 --> 00:16:27,985
blowing up the second floor
solely to trick Miss Adler.

291
00:16:28,027 --> 00:16:30,488
I thought you were mad
when you first proposed it,

292
00:16:30,530 --> 00:16:32,365
but it seems to have worked!

293
00:16:32,407 --> 00:16:34,950
And Miss Hudson's
performance was stellar.

294
00:16:34,992 --> 00:16:36,619
Well, after the two
of you blew up my home,

295
00:16:36,661 --> 00:16:38,830
I wasn't about to mess that up!

296
00:16:38,872 --> 00:16:44,085
You... set this up?
To go this far. Why?

297
00:16:44,126 --> 00:16:45,169
It's simple.

298
00:16:45,211 --> 00:16:48,673
Because we wanted to
protect you, Miss Adler.

299
00:16:48,715 --> 00:16:51,133
Protect? How so?

300
00:16:51,175 --> 00:16:53,386
[HOLMES] I knew there was
something else motivating you

301
00:16:53,428 --> 00:16:56,389
aside from the photo
of the King of Bohemia.

302
00:16:56,431 --> 00:16:59,684
Which implied that the king's
request was meaningless.

303
00:16:59,726 --> 00:17:02,687
It stood to reason that the
king's photo was a red herring.

304
00:17:02,729 --> 00:17:05,106
Which would mean, your real
objective

305
00:17:05,147 --> 00:17:07,191
was to work your way close to
me.

306
00:17:07,233 --> 00:17:10,152
Which seemed unfathomable at
first.

307
00:17:10,194 --> 00:17:12,113
Foregoing my powers
of deduction,

308
00:17:12,154 --> 00:17:13,531
as much as I hate to say it,

309
00:17:13,573 --> 00:17:17,911
my current level of popularity
is what you found most useful.

310
00:17:17,952 --> 00:17:20,329
Someone will find it
difficult to move against you,

311
00:17:20,371 --> 00:17:21,330
while you're in the orbit

312
00:17:21,372 --> 00:17:23,999
of one of London's
most famous citizens.

313
00:17:24,041 --> 00:17:27,086
Which suggests, whoever
is coming for you--

314
00:17:27,128 --> 00:17:29,004
they aren't the mafia
or the nobility,

315
00:17:29,046 --> 00:17:31,173
who would stop at nothing
to deal with the matter.

316
00:17:31,215 --> 00:17:35,887
So, why is the British
government out to get you?

317
00:17:35,929 --> 00:17:37,889
Hoping to bring that
reason to light,

318
00:17:37,931 --> 00:17:40,057
we pretended to
come under attack,

319
00:17:40,099 --> 00:17:42,435
and you fell for it.

320
00:17:42,477 --> 00:17:44,979
Now, what could this be?

321
00:17:45,020 --> 00:17:46,731
Stop, Sherlock!

322
00:17:46,773 --> 00:17:49,233
If you look,
there's no going back!

323
00:17:49,275 --> 00:17:52,320
They're just going to come after
you, like they came for me!

324
00:17:52,361 --> 00:17:56,073
Hah. I destroyed my own room
to get my hands on this secret.

325
00:17:56,115 --> 00:18:00,620
You think I'm concerned
about who might come after me?

326
00:18:00,662 --> 00:18:02,789
[LOUIS]
I'm sorry, tactical plans?

327
00:18:02,831 --> 00:18:06,626
[ALBERT] Yes. 100 years ago,
the British Empire,

328
00:18:06,668 --> 00:18:09,128
had a hand in manipulating
the French Revolution

329
00:18:09,170 --> 00:18:10,463
from the shadows.

330
00:18:10,505 --> 00:18:12,047
Those were the plans for it.

331
00:18:12,089 --> 00:18:15,092
What? Why was there
a need for that?

332
00:18:15,134 --> 00:18:17,720
[WILLIAM]
It's quite simple, Louis.

333
00:18:17,762 --> 00:18:19,722
It was an experiment.

334
00:18:19,764 --> 00:18:22,600
The purpose of which was
to see what would happen

335
00:18:22,642 --> 00:18:24,018
if an oppressive monarchy

336
00:18:24,059 --> 00:18:26,771
were to switch to
a republic of the common man.

337
00:18:26,813 --> 00:18:28,063
Well,

338
00:18:28,105 --> 00:18:30,775
it's possible the British
government's plan had always

339
00:18:30,817 --> 00:18:34,195
been intended to trigger a
bloody revolution of some sort.

340
00:18:34,236 --> 00:18:36,614
It would at least throw
the ensuing French government

341
00:18:36,656 --> 00:18:40,785
into a cascading
state of turmoil.

342
00:18:40,827 --> 00:18:42,328
Whatever the case,

343
00:18:42,370 --> 00:18:47,000
it seems, we must retrieve
those papers post-haste.

344
00:18:47,041 --> 00:18:51,253
From this moment on,
there's no turning back for us.

345
00:18:56,509 --> 00:18:58,511
How the devil did
you get your hands on

346
00:18:58,553 --> 00:19:00,555
a document this dangerous?

347
00:19:00,596 --> 00:19:03,432
I might have stolen it
from Buckingham Palace.

348
00:19:03,474 --> 00:19:07,144
Though I never would have taken
it if I knew what was inside.

349
00:19:07,186 --> 00:19:09,647
I just needed something in
order to blackmail the prince,

350
00:19:09,689 --> 00:19:10,899
that's all.

351
00:19:10,940 --> 00:19:14,485
[WATSON grunts] You intended
to blackmail the prince?

352
00:19:14,527 --> 00:19:16,779
To what end, exactly?

353
00:19:16,821 --> 00:19:19,532
[ADLER]
My goal was to reform the world.

354
00:19:19,574 --> 00:19:22,869
It's what I have always done.

355
00:19:22,911 --> 00:19:25,914
It started when an actress
I knew was driven to

356
00:19:25,955 --> 00:19:29,458
take her own life simply because
she was from a common family.

357
00:19:29,500 --> 00:19:32,879
No matter how hard she worked,
she was never given her due.

358
00:19:32,921 --> 00:19:36,090
I urged the nobility, who had
been criticising her so cruelly,

359
00:19:36,131 --> 00:19:38,384
to see things in
a different light.

360
00:19:38,426 --> 00:19:40,094
I used my feminine allure to

361
00:19:40,135 --> 00:19:43,431
ingratiate myself into
their circle, and from there,

362
00:19:43,472 --> 00:19:46,392
exploited the upper
class's weaknesses.

363
00:19:46,434 --> 00:19:49,896
And so, I supported those who
would not have moved ahead

364
00:19:49,938 --> 00:19:52,147
due to their social status.

365
00:19:52,189 --> 00:19:53,942
I worked to ensure
that their talents

366
00:19:53,983 --> 00:19:56,569
would one day bear fruit.

367
00:19:56,611 --> 00:19:57,612
You expect us to believe

368
00:19:57,653 --> 00:20:00,281
you did something
that grand by yourself?

369
00:20:00,322 --> 00:20:03,158
[HOLMES]
No, John. It's the truth.

370
00:20:03,200 --> 00:20:08,456
Having heard this my profile
of Miss Adler finally fits.

371
00:20:08,497 --> 00:20:09,958
With leverage on the prince,

372
00:20:09,999 --> 00:20:12,376
I had a chance to
change this country.

373
00:20:12,418 --> 00:20:17,172
But instead, I stole something
too big for me to handle.

374
00:20:17,214 --> 00:20:18,883
I'm a fool.

375
00:20:18,925 --> 00:20:21,970
And then I dragged all
of you into this, as well.

376
00:20:24,847 --> 00:20:27,016
I'm sorry.

377
00:20:27,058 --> 00:20:28,768
That's just bad luck.

378
00:20:28,810 --> 00:20:32,229
So, care to tell us where
you've been all evening?

379
00:20:36,358 --> 00:20:38,778
[ADLER] The government
promised to spare me...

380
00:20:38,820 --> 00:20:40,571
...on the condition
that I turn over the papers

381
00:20:40,613 --> 00:20:42,782
you're currently holding.

382
00:20:42,824 --> 00:20:46,535
As well as all the
information I've obtained.

383
00:20:46,577 --> 00:20:50,665
Don't you see? Without that
folder, my life is over!

384
00:20:50,706 --> 00:20:53,375
Sherlock, please.

385
00:20:59,340 --> 00:21:00,883
[HOLMES grunts]

386
00:21:10,768 --> 00:21:12,645
Are you sure about
this, Sherlock?

387
00:21:12,687 --> 00:21:14,605
Giving her the pages?

388
00:21:14,647 --> 00:21:16,524
[HOLMES groans]

389
00:21:16,565 --> 00:21:19,027
That's it! I've
had enough of this!

390
00:21:19,068 --> 00:21:23,656
[groans] Our options
are running thin and yet,

391
00:21:23,698 --> 00:21:26,034
what she said is clearly untrue!

392
00:21:26,075 --> 00:21:28,619
[WATSON gasps]
What are you on about?

393
00:21:28,661 --> 00:21:32,498
What Irene said about reasoning
with the government was untrue.

394
00:21:32,540 --> 00:21:34,167
I said it before.

395
00:21:34,208 --> 00:21:38,880
The government--no, he  has never
been one to do things so simply.

396
00:21:38,921 --> 00:21:41,256
Which means our
con-artist courtesan

397
00:21:41,298 --> 00:21:42,967
is dealing with someone else!

398
00:21:43,009 --> 00:21:46,054
Really? But who could it be?

399
00:21:46,095 --> 00:21:48,305
[HOLMES]
Elementary, my dear Watson.

400
00:21:48,347 --> 00:21:51,100
Who would want such
a godforsaken document

401
00:21:51,142 --> 00:21:53,769
and have enough
influence to protect Irene

402
00:21:53,811 --> 00:21:55,688
from the clutches
of the government?

403
00:21:55,730 --> 00:21:59,984
Yes, Irene has made a deal with

404
00:22:00,026 --> 00:22:02,611
the Lord of Crime.

405
00:23:34,787 --> 00:23:36,872
[LOUIS] Next time on
Moriarty the Patriot  :

406
00:23:36,914 --> 00:23:40,042
"A Scandal in the
British Empire Act 3."
